send_cmd: limit the reply length to the reply buffer

send_cmd_rx takes the number of reply bytes from the client and handed it to
read_string() unchecked: read_string() first clears that many bytes of the
512 byte reply buffer and then fills them, so a large length overwrote the
stack and a negative one, taken as a size, ran off it. Without a length the
size was BUFSZ + 1: one byte past the buffer on every send_cmd.

diff --git a/tests/rigctl_parse.c b/tests/rigctl_parse.c
--- a/tests/rigctl_parse.c
+++ b/tests/rigctl_parse.c
@@ -4922,8 +4922,12 @@ declare_proto_rig(send_cmd)
         if (arg2[0] == ';') { eom_buf[0] = ';'; }
 
         /* Assumes CR or LF is end of line char for all ASCII protocols. */
-        retval = read_string(&rs->rigport, buf, rxbytes, eom_buf,
-                             strlen(eom_buf), 0, 1);
+        /* [RADE] rxbytes is the client's, and read_string() clears and then
+         * fills as many bytes as it is told: never more than buf holds.
+         * (Without a length it was BUFSZ + 1: one byte past buf, every time.) */
+        retval = read_string(&rs->rigport, buf,
+                             rxbytes < 0 ? 0 : rxbytes > BUFSZ ? BUFSZ : rxbytes,
+                             eom_buf, strlen(eom_buf), 0, 1);
 
         if (retval < 0)
         {
